      {
        type: "quiz",
        title: "Knowledge Check",
        question: "A hospital receives a letter from a contractor demanding records for 75 inpatient stays from 2 years ago. The letter says the contractor will be paid a contingency fee based on the dollar value of overpayments identified. Which type of contractor is this?",
        options: [
          { text: "MAC (Medicare Administrative Contractor)", correct: false },
          { text: "RAC (Recovery Audit Contractor)", correct: true },
          { text: "ZPIC/UPIC (Unified Program Integrity Contractor)", correct: false },
          { text: "QIO (Quality Improvement Organization)", correct: false }
        ],
        explanation: `The **RAC (Recovery Audit Contractor)** is the only Medicare contractor paid on a **contingency fee** basis tied to **dollar value of overpayments** identified. RACs have a **3-year lookback** from the date of payment and review for both overpayments and underpayments (though underpayments are rarely flagged in practice). **MACs** are paid **fixed contracts** to process claims and conduct medical review. **ZPICs/UPICs** focus on **fraud investigation** and make **criminal referrals** — they are not paid based on overpayment recoveries. **QIOs** focus on quality of care, not financial overpayments. The contingency-fee detail is the unmistakable RAC signature.`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="70" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="36" font-weight="bold">RAC — The Signature Clues</text><rect x="100" y="150" width="900" height="120" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.25" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="3" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="195" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="26" font-weight="bold">Clue 1: Contingency Fee</text><text x="550" y="235" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Paid % of recovery → only RACs work this way</text><rect x="100" y="290" width="900" height="120" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.25" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="3" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="335" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="26" font-weight="bold">Clue 2: 3-Year Lookback</text><text x="550" y="375" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">From date of payment, retrospective only</text><rect x="100" y="430" width="900" height="120" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.25" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="3" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="475" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="26" font-weight="bold">Clue 3: ADR (Additional Documentation Request)</text><text x="550" y="515" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Records request, not site visit</text><rect x="100" y="600" width="900" height="400" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.15" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="645" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="24" font-weight="bold">How to Tell Them Apart</text><text x="150" y="695" fill="#10b981" font-size="20" font-weight="bold">MAC:</text><text x="280" y="695" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">Processes claims daily, prepayment TPE</text><text x="150" y="740" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="20" font-weight="bold">RAC:</text><text x="280" y="740" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">Post-payment, contingency, ADRs, 3 years</text><text x="150" y="785" fill="#ef4444" font-size="20" font-weight="bold">UPIC:</text><text x="280" y="785" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">Fraud focus, criminal referrals, site visits</text><text x="150" y="830" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="20" font-weight="bold">QIO:</text><text x="280" y="830" fill="#ffffff" font-size="20">Quality of care, not financial</text><text x="550" y="900" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ec4899" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Memory hook:</text><text x="550" y="945" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">RAC = "Recovery on Commission"</text></svg>`,
          caption: "Three diagnostic clues that identify a RAC request"
        }
      },
      {
        type: "application",
        title: "On the Job: Audit-Proofing the Coder's Workflow",
        content: `**Daily coder habits** that survive RAC, MAC, and UPIC scrutiny: (1) **Query, don't assume** — every time documentation is **ambiguous, incomplete, or contradictory**, send a **compliant physician query** (open-ended, multiple-choice if ≥2 reasonable options, no leading language). (2) **Reference the Work Plan quarterly** — pull the OIG focus areas, audit a small sample of your facility's recent claims in those categories. (3) **Code from the record, not the claim** — never code based on what the chargemaster suggests or what physicians "usually do." (4) **Document your coding rationale** for high-dollar or complex cases — if a RAC pulls it 18 months later, your notes are the defense. (5) **Track denial patterns** by payer and reason — recurring denials in the same code/modifier combo are a **leading indicator** that an audit is coming. (6) **Respond to ADRs within deadlines** — a missed deadline = automatic loss.`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="34" font-weight="bold">Coder's Audit-Proof Workflow</text><rect x="50" y="110" width="1000" height="900" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.1" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2" rx="15"/><rect x="80" y="140" width="940" height="130" fill="#6366f1" opacity="0.25" rx="10"/><text x="100" y="180" fill="#6366f1" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">1. Query, don't assume</text><text x="100" y="215" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">Ambiguous, incomplete, or contradictory documentation</text><text x="100" y="245" fill="#ffd700" font-size="18">→ compliant query (open-ended, non-leading)</text><rect x="80" y="285" width="940" height="130" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.25" rx="10"/><text x="100" y="325" fill="#10b981" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">2. Reference Work Plan quarterly</text><text x="100" y="360" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">Pull OIG focus areas every 90 days</text><text x="100" y="390" fill="#ffd700" font-size="18">→ small internal sample audit in flagged categories</text><rect x="80" y="430" width="940" height="130" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.25" rx="10"/><text x="100" y="470" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">3. Code from the record only</text><text x="100" y="505" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">Ignore chargemaster nudges and "what we always do"</text><text x="100" y="535" fill="#ffd700" font-size="18">→ documentation is the only valid source</text><rect x="80" y="575" width="940" height="130" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.25" rx="10"/><text x="100" y="615" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">4. Document your rationale</text><text x="100" y="650" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">For complex or high-dollar codes, note your reasoning</text><text x="100" y="680" fill="#ffd700" font-size="18">→ your defense 18 months later in a RAC review</text><rect x="80" y="720" width="940" height="130" fill="#ec4899" opacity="0.25" rx="10"/><text x="100" y="760" fill="#ec4899" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">5. Track denial patterns</text><text x="100" y="795" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">By payer + code + modifier combination</text><text x="100" y="825" fill="#ffd700" font-size="18">→ recurring denials predict next audit</text><rect x="80" y="865" width="940" height="130" fill="#ef4444" opacity="0.25" rx="10"/><text x="100" y="905" fill="#ef4444" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">6. Respond to ADRs on time</text><text x="100" y="940" fill="#ffffff" font-size="18">45 days for most contractor record requests</text><text x="100" y="970" fill="#ffd700" font-size="18">→ missed deadline = automatic overpayment</text></svg>`,
          caption: "Six daily habits that keep coders ahead of auditors"
        }
      }
